{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And they said, etc., to stone, etc.</b> One needs to know why they were not aroused by the first statement that Caleb made: ״עָלֹה נַעֲלֶה וְיָרַשְׁנוּ וְגוֹ׳ נוּכַל לָהּ״. And it appears that they were not concerned about Caleb's words, because his words are nullified on their own, since the testimony of a single witness does not stand against the testimony of many, as it is written (Deuteronomy 19:15) ״עַל פִּי שְׁנֵי עֵדִים יָקוּם דָּבָר״ — therefore they paid no heed to his words. This was not so, however, when the words of Joshua and Caleb came together: now the testimony of the spies was invalidated, for the law of two witnesses is equivalent to the law of a hundred witnesses (Makkot 5a), and this testimony was balanced [equally on both sides], so there was testimony neither of the spies nor of Joshua and Caleb, and the case reverted to what it had been before the sending of the spies. Therefore they were concerned about them and said to stone them." "<b>And one needs</b> to know why they were not astute enough to judge according to the law of the Torah to contradict the words of the ten with the words of the two, given that the law of testimony is תְּרֵי כְּמֵאָה וּמֵאָה כִּתְרֵי (two are like a hundred, and a hundred are like two). And it is possible that they disqualified Joshua from testifying in matters pertaining to Moses, for he was his attendant from his youth."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:08:51.284714442Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}